<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>RytivoAPNsEnvironment</key><string>$(APNS_SERVER_ENVIRONMENT)</string>
    <key>NSSupportsLiveActivities</key><true/>
    <key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
    <array><dict>
        <key>CFBundleURLName</key><string>com.pbllc.rytivo.widgets</string>
        <key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key><array><string>rytivo</string></array>
    </dict></array>
	<!--
	  Export compliance. Without this key every single TestFlight and App
	  Store upload stops and asks the same question by hand before the build
	  can go anywhere, which is a slow way to answer "no" forty times.

	  false is the answer because the only cryptography here is the system's:
	  HTTPS through URLSession, and the Keychain holding the session token.
	  Apple exempts both. It would have to become true if the app ever did
	  its own encryption: shipped its own algorithm, or encrypted files
	  itself before storing or sending them.

	  This is a declaration to a regulator, not a build setting. Re-read it
	  if that ever changes.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<!--
	  CFBundleName is deliberately NOT here. It is set by PRODUCT_NAME in the
	  project file, because that is the only thing that actually sets it, and
	  finding that out cost two wrong attempts worth recording:

	    - written in this file, it loses the merge. Xcode's generated plist
	      carries CFBundleName = $(PRODUCT_NAME) and overwrites the value.
	    - written as INFOPLIST_KEY_CFBundleName, it is silently dropped;
	      the same trap that made every release build die on launch, because
	      INFOPLIST_KEY_ only works for the keys Xcode knows, and it does not
	      know that one.

	  Both were built and read back out of the .app before PRODUCT_NAME was
	  touched. PRODUCT_NAME is also why the built bundle is Rytivo.app rather
	  than "IOS Frontend.app"; the paths in CI and in
	  Scripts/archive-for-testflight.sh follow it.
	-->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
		<false/>
		<key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
	<!--
	  Which server a release build talks to. APIConfiguration reads this and
	  refuses to run without an https value, so it is what stands between a
	  shipped build and one quietly pointing at a laptop.

	  It has to be spelled here rather than as INFOPLIST_KEY_REPBASE_API_URL,
	  which is what the project used to carry and what made every release
	  build die on launch: INFOPLIST_KEY_* only works for the keys Xcode
	  knows about, so a custom one is silently dropped and never reaches the
	  built plist. Written this way Xcode expands the build setting, and CI
	  checks that it still does.
	-->
	<key>REPBASE_API_URL</key>
	<string>$(REPBASE_API_URL)</string>
	<!--
	  Xcode merges this file with the keys generated from the
	  INFOPLIST_KEY_* build settings. It holds only what the generator
	  cannot express: UIBackgroundModes is required for location updates
	  to continue while a run, ride, or swim session is in progress and
	  the screen is locked.
	-->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>location</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIAppFonts</key>
	<array>
		<string>NunitoSans-Regular.ttf</string>
		<string>NunitoSans-SemiBold.ttf</string>
		<string>NunitoSans-Bold.ttf</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  The barometric altimeter is read through CoreMotion, which requires
	  this description. It measures height change far more precisely than
	  GPS, so climb during a run or ride is worth reporting.
	-->
	<!--
	  Asked only when the user has Precise Location switched off and starts a
	  session. Without exact positions there is no route to draw at all: every
	  fix a coarse location produces is kilometres wide and fails the tracker's
	  accuracy filter, so the session would record an empty track.

	  The key inside the dictionary must match the purpose key passed to
	  requestTemporaryFullAccuracyAuthorization, or iOS refuses to ask.
	-->
	<key>NSLocationTemporaryUsageDescriptionDictionary</key>
	<dict>
		<key>RouteTracking</key>
		<string>Rytivo needs your exact location to map the route of this run, ride, or swim and to measure its distance. Without it the session can still be timed, but no route can be recorded.</string>
	</dict>
	<key>NSMotionUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Rytivo reads your device's barometer during a run, ride, or swim to measure the elevation you climb. It is only used while a session you started is running.</string>
	<!--
	  HealthKit. Reading only, and only this key.
	  NSHealthUpdateUsageDescription was here too, added on the belief that
	  an app linking HealthKit needs both or is killed at launch. Only writing
	  needs it: removing it was tried, and the app launches and reads fine.

	  Apple's sheet still reads "access and update your Health data" either
	  way. That header is Apple's own boilerplate, not something this file
	  controls; the part that reflects the request says "Allow to read" and
	  lists only Steps.
	-->
	<key>NSHealthShareUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Rytivo reads your step count and the runs, rides, and swims recorded by your iPhone or Apple Watch, so your training shows up here without entering it twice.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
